Golden-file testing setup for a table formatter. Create /workspace/tablefmt/tablefmt.py:

def render_table(rows, headers):
    widths = [len(h) for h in headers]
    for row in rows:
        for i, cell in enumerate(row):
            widths[i] = max(widths[i], len(str(cell)))
    def line(cells):
        return "| " + " | ".join(str(c).ljust(widths[i]) for i, c in enumerate(cells)) + " |"
    sep = "+-" + "-+-".join("-" * w for w in widths) + "-+"
    out = [sep, line(headers), sep]
    for row in rows:
        out.append(line(row))
    out.append(sep)
    return "\n".join(out)

Build a golden-file harness around it: tests/golden/ holds .txt expected outputs; tests/test_golden.py auto-discovers case functions from tests/cases.py (each returns (name, rows, headers)), renders, and compares to tests/golden/<name>.txt with a unified diff in the assertion message on mismatch; when the env var UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 is set, it (re)writes the golden files instead of asserting and marks the test as skipped with reason updated. Provide at least 5 cases: basic, unicode widths (note that str.ljust counts codepoints, not display width; pick pure-ASCII vs accented cases and freeze current behavior), numeric right-vs-left (frozen as left), empty rows list, single column. Workflow to demonstrate in order: run tests (fail, no goldens), UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 pytest to generate, plain pytest green, then intentionally tweak the separator character in the code, show the diff output in the failure, revert, green again. Also add a guard test that fails if a golden file exists with no matching case (orphan detection).

